 hook='Plate empty in 3 minutes flat! 🍜💨 What if you ate super fast every single day? KOMO explains why your stomach and brain might not keep up.',
 how='When you eat, your stomach and gut release hormones that tell your brain "I am getting full". That message takes time, often around 20 minutes, to fully arrive. If you eat very fast, you can finish a big meal before the "full" signal reaches your brain, so you keep eating more than you need. Fast eating also means less chewing and more swallowed air.',
 facts=['Fullness signals from your gut to your brain take time to build up.',
        'A large review found fast eaters were more likely to gain weight than slow eaters.',
        'Eating slowly is linked to eating fewer calories at a meal in several studies.',
        'Chewing more helps break down food and gives your body more time to feel full.',
        'Eating very fast can cause bloating, burping and heartburn for some people.',
        'Distractions like phones and TV can make you eat faster without noticing.'],
 tips_head='SLOW DOWN TRICKS',
